line by line.

cue0090264
01:11:40.980 --> 01:11:43.480
What the Ḥakham wrote in an appositional phrase

cue0090265
01:11:43.480 --> 01:11:44.920
is very important,

cue0090266
01:11:45.220 --> 01:11:46.740
because the word revelation,

cue0090267
01:11:47.120 --> 01:11:49.160
the word we use in English, is a goy word.

cue0090268
01:11:50.180 --> 01:11:53.000
What is the Tora equivalent of this goy word?

cue0090269
01:11:53.580 --> 01:11:54.900
It means how Boreʾ ʿOlam

cue0090270
01:11:54.900 --> 01:11:57.840
communicates to human beings.

cue0090271
01:11:57.840 --> 01:12:04.080
Now, it appears in Scripture and in Maimonidean intellectual apparatus.

cue0090272
01:12:04.080 --> 01:12:05.720
What is the Ramnam doing?

cue0090273
01:12:05.720 --> 01:12:11.380
In Maureen al-Bukhi, he's telling us how to read Kitray Qadosh.

cue0090274
01:12:11.380 --> 01:12:16.080
And as I cited the source in Yefot HaShuvah, one looks at this.

cue0090275
01:12:16.080 --> 01:12:17.080
What does it mean in Scripture?

cue0090276
01:12:17.080 --> 01:12:20.920
Vayi Yom Yud Kevam Kei El Moshe.

cue0090277
01:12:20.920 --> 01:12:22.580
He's talking to Moses.

cue0090278
01:12:22.580 --> 01:12:24.080
What the word Amirah there means?

cue0090279
01:12:24.080 --> 01:12:27.000
It could mean talk, it could mean committing a vow.

cue0090280
01:12:27.000 --> 01:12:32.200
somehow what's on the mind giving a full of Boreʾ ʿolam is somehow getting into the mind of Moshe,

cue0090281
01:12:32.840 --> 01:12:40.040
who then transmits it to Aaron, as is the Jewish people, through various series of repetitions.

cue0090282
01:12:41.080 --> 01:12:47.080
And this is how we become accessible, that God becomes accessible to us. Although God is as he

cue0090283
01:12:47.080 --> 01:12:55.000
is himself, our interest is Dios es candidus, that means Boreʾ ʿolam is totally other. It's a realm

cue0090284
01:12:55.000 --> 01:13:00.680
is not reducible to anything in human language. And Maimonides explains this in

cue0090285
01:13:00.680 --> 01:13:07.120
as I understand, in the following way. He says, anything that you say about

cue0090286
01:13:07.120 --> 01:13:13.820
Goethe-Lacke, in human language, in a positive way, has to be wrong. Because it is

cue0090287
01:13:13.820 --> 01:13:18.820
totally different kind of concept. For example, there is a Goethe-Philosopher,

cue0090288
01:13:18.820 --> 01:13:23.560
actually two Goethe-Philosophers, G.E. Moore and Bertrand Russell, who spoke about what are

cue0090289
01:13:23.560 --> 01:13:24.960
called category errors.

cue0090290
01:13:25.760 --> 01:13:27.900
If I try to divide a number by zero,

cue0090291
01:13:28.460 --> 01:13:29.500
it just doesn't make sense.

cue0090292
01:13:30.220 --> 01:13:31.560
To use human language

cue0090293
01:13:31.560 --> 01:13:33.600
doesn't make sense when you talk about God

cue0090294
01:13:33.600 --> 01:13:35.440
in human language. Why?

cue0090295
01:13:36.420 --> 01:13:38.040
Every word that we use,

cue0090296
01:13:39.160 --> 01:13:39.540
tie,

cue0090297
01:13:40.480 --> 01:13:41.120
blue hat,

cue0090298
01:13:41.940 --> 01:13:42.860
big briefcase,

cue0090299
01:13:43.640 --> 01:13:45.600
big belly, is part

cue0090300
01:13:45.600 --> 01:13:47.040
of our experience.

cue0090301
01:13:48.360 --> 01:13:49.560
For us to

cue0090302
01:13:49.560 --> 01:13:51.700
assign that

cue0090303
01:13:51.700 --> 01:13:53.540
experience that we have to Borealon,

cue0090304
01:13:53.560 --> 01:13:55.760
is the height of hubris.

cue0090305
01:13:56.240 --> 01:13:56.580
Ga'avan.

cue0090306
01:13:57.200 --> 01:13:58.160
What does it mean, ga'avan?

cue0090307
01:13:59.100 --> 01:14:00.780
We're taking our experience,

cue0090308
01:14:01.220 --> 01:14:02.820
and I'm putting our experience on Bar-El-Lam,

cue0090309
01:14:03.340 --> 01:14:04.940
and saying, Mr. God, you've got to live

cue0090310
01:14:04.940 --> 01:14:06.620
your life as God,

cue0090311
01:14:07.380 --> 01:14:09.420
based on our experience as human beings,

cue0090312
01:14:10.080 --> 01:14:11.960
which is created by our human language.

cue0090313
01:14:12.760 --> 01:14:14.280
When we're told by Ḥakhamim

cue0090314
01:14:14.280 --> 01:14:16.600
that the Tora speaks to the Shomrei ʾAdam,

cue0090315
01:14:17.340 --> 01:14:19.520
it's somehow to take the infinite

cue0090316
01:14:19.520 --> 01:14:21.600
and crystallize it in human language,

cue0090317
01:14:21.600 --> 01:14:24.460
which means sometimes it's not meant to be literal.

cue0090318
01:14:24.620 --> 01:14:26.400
Example, when we say in Keri Hashema,

cue0090319
01:14:26.920 --> 01:14:31.400
literally means God's nose gets hot.

cue0090320
01:14:32.400 --> 01:14:33.840
Now God doesn't have a nose,

cue0090321
01:14:34.240 --> 01:14:35.340
because God doesn't have a body.

cue0090322
01:14:35.840 --> 01:14:37.280
So what does it mean God's nose gets hot?

cue0090323
01:14:37.780 --> 01:14:39.900
We're using a human metaphor

cue0090324
01:14:39.900 --> 01:14:43.720
to explain how if,

cue0090325
01:14:44.720 --> 01:14:47.340
and it is that somehow makes sense to us,

cue0090326
01:14:47.340 --> 01:14:50.280
the Kori ʿOlam is, to use a human word,

cue0090327
01:14:50.280 --> 01:14:51.860
unhappy with us.

cue0090328
01:14:52.720 --> 01:14:54.380
I give example in the

cue0090329
01:14:54.380 --> 01:14:56.140
shiur I gave last night

cue0090330
01:14:56.140 --> 01:14:57.760
to the ladies in my synagogue.

cue0090331
01:14:58.920 --> 01:14:59.760
What does it mean

cue0090332
01:14:59.760 --> 01:15:01.240
that Boreal

cue0090333
01:15:01.240 --> 01:15:05.040
regretted

cue0090334
01:15:05.040 --> 01:15:06.340
that made humankind?

cue0090335
01:15:07.760 --> 01:15:08.320
I asked

cue0090336
01:15:08.320 --> 01:15:09.980
Rabbi Fa'ur to explain that to me

cue0090337
01:15:09.980 --> 01:15:11.760
and he refused.

cue0090338
01:15:11.760 --> 01:15:13.780
He says, you have to

cue0090339
01:15:13.780 --> 01:15:15.540
